Note, importantly, that imposing the causality constraint does not shift the CstR_{\\rm NS} posterior PDF to higher radii (Figure 2), but simply rejects parts of the M_{\\rm NS}-R_{\\rm NS} parameter M_{\\rm NS}-R_{\\rm NS} space. The existence of large-mass NSs (Demorest et al., 2010; Antoniadis et al., 2013) combined with the causality condition leads to a minimum acceptable value of R_{\\rm NS}. In other words, the effect of larger mass NSs being detected is to exclude the part of the CstR_{\\rm NS} posterior PDF below the corresponding R_{\\rm caus}. For example, for M_{\\rm NS}=2.01\\,M_{\\odot}, R_{\\rm caus}=8.40 km excludes R_{\\rm NS} parameter space below this value (see Figure 1). As a consequence, there is significant tension between the CstR_{\\rm NS} result and high-mass NSs. Therefore, a definitive measurement of a 2.6\\,M_{\\odot} NS - within the uncertainty of a 2.40\\pm 0.12\\,M_{\\odot} pulsar (van Kerkwijk et al., 2011) - would rule out a CstR_{\\rm NS} radius <11.0 km, essentially all the R_{\\rm NS} acceptable parameter space obtained here. This would falsify the conclusions of this work, and therefore one or more of the assumptions made.

## 4 Testing proposed dEOSs

Here, it is measured to what degree of certainty a selection of popular proposed dEOSs fit the spectra of the six qLMXBs in the sample. This spectral analysis is performed with the MCMC analysis described above by forcing the fitted M_{\\rm NS} and R_{\\rm NS} parameters to be constrained to the M_{\\rm NS}(R_{\\rm NS}) curve of a user-defined dEOS. To do so, the MCMC-sampled parameters are not the M_{\\rm NS} and R_{\\rm NS} of each NSs, but instead a parameter between 0 and 1 representing the position on the dEOS curve, with 0 at the tip (at M_{\\rm max}), and 1 at the M_{\\rm NS}=0.5\\,M_{\\odot} point (see posterior PDFs in Figure 3).

To determine which dEOS is quantitatively preferred by the data, five dEOSs with varied stiffness are tested, as well as three representative dEOSs obtained from chiral effective field theory (Hebeler et al., 2013): the Soft, Intermediate and Stiff representative dEOSs (labeled CEFT1, CEFT2, and CEFT3 here). For each dEOS, the minimum \\chi^{2} and the corresponding null hypothesis probability (NHP) are obtained after convergence of the MCMC simulation. The NHP is the probability of finding by chance a \\chi^{2} as large or larger as the minimum \\chi^{2} found if the model is correct. Results are shown in Table 2 and Figure 1. The MCMC runs testing the eight dEOSs have acceptance rates in the range \\sim 6-9\\%. In addition, one can notice that the NHP decreases as the average radius, i.e. the stiffness, of the tested dEOS, increases.

To the reader, there may appear to be a discrepancy between the NHPs obtained when testing dEOSs and the likelihood distributions of the CstR_{\\rm NS} case (Figure 1, _top_). Specifically, on the one hand, the posterior PDFs of R_{\\rm NS} indicates that R_{\\rm NS}=14\\,{\\rm km} is \\sim 6\\sigma away from the median of the distribution. On the other hand, the test for MSO (with average radius \\sim 14\\,{\\rm km}) gives a NHP of 0.003, i.e., MS0 is rejected at the 99.7% confidence (\\sim3\\sigma).

This is better illustrated with Figure 1 (_bottom_) showing the 2D distribution of the \\chi^{2}_{\\nu} value as a function of R_{\\rm NS} for all accepted MCMC steps of the CstR_{\\rm NS} analysis (no causality assumed), revealing the extent of the radius coverage. The plot also shows the minimum \\chi^{2}_{\\nu} (red) obtained when the R_{\\rm NS} is fixed to a selection of values (7-16 km), essentially a selection of toy model dEOSs. The minimum \\chi^{2}_{\\nu} of the 2D distribution follows the minimum \\chi^{2}_{\\nu} of the red line in the range 7-11 km, but becomes systematically larger above 12 km and disappears above 13 km. This apparent difference (between the Bayesian-accepted parameter space and the minimum \\chi^{2}_{\\nu} values at each R_{\\rm NS}) is similar to the difference between confidence levels described in the previous paragraph. This emerges as a consequence of the complicated parameter space above 12 km in the CstR_{\\rm NS} run; the probability of excursion to radii larger than 12 km during the MCMC run is low since such steps are more likely to be rejected, making it difficult for the MCMC to populate this area of the parameter space.

This justification is investigated with the rejected MCMC steps in the CstR_{\\rm NS} analysis. As the R_{\\rm NS} goes to larger values, the NS masses are forced to smaller values to maintain the R_{\\infty} values that fits the spectral data of each NS. A constant R_{\\infty}=R_{\\rm NS}\\left(1-2GM_{\\rm NS}/R_{\\rm NS}\\,c^{2}\\right)^{-1/2} implies for large R_{\\rm NS}, smaller M_{\\rm NS}. As a result, an increasing proportion of MCMC steps are rejected because the mass parameters are more likely to lie outside the allowed range of values (0.5-3.0 M_{\\odot}, the hard limits of the nsatmos model). A significant fraction (93.2%) of the proposed steps with R_{\\rm NS}>13\\,{\\rm km} are rejected simply due to one or more M_{\\rm NS,i} being outside the prior limits. For comparison, when all radii are considered, only Figure 3: Figure showing the posterior distributions in M_{\\rm NS}–R_{\\rm NS} space for all the tested dEOSs. Each curve is colored by the value of the PDFs (different for each dEOSs, therefore the color bar only shows “Min” and “Max”). In the MCMC runs testing dEOS models, the M_{\\rm NS} and R_{\\rm NS} are constrained to remain on the dEOS with the use of a position parameter sampling the M_{\\rm NS}(R_{\\rm NS}) curve between 0.5M_{\\odot} and M_{\\rm max}. Therefore, the priors for M_{\\rm NS} and R_{\\rm NS} are the M_{\\rm NS}(R_{\\rm NS}) curves of the dEOSs.

40.4% of proposed steps are rejected due to one or more M_{\\rm NS,\\it i} being outside the hard limits. Interestingly, we note that a larger minimum M_{\\rm NS} value (assumed from theory) would result in an even lower maximum radius from this Bayesian analysis.

To further test that the observation illustrated in Figure 1 (_bottom_) is a consequence of the shape of the parameter space, a separate CstR_{\\rm NS} trial is performed, in which all MCMC walkers were initiated with R_{\\rm NS}>13 km. The walkers are all observed to "move" and converge into the final parameter space of Figure 1 (_bottom_), with all R_{\\rm NS}<13 km, as expected from the MCMC algorithm.

The "noisy" parameter space occurs because of the strong covariance between the 32 model parameters in the CstR_{\\rm NS} analysis. Overall, the apparent difference between the acceptable parameter space from the MCMC analysis and the \\chi^{2} analysis is due to the fact that Bayesian and \\chi^{2} statistics rely on different assumptions about the statistical system. This underscores the need for a Monte Carlo integration to assess most-likely parameters and uncertainty regions, rather than relying upon the \\chi^{2} curvature matrix.

Overall, this minimum \\chi^{2} values obtained for each dEOS tested in this analysis, and their corresponding NHPs, provide a quantitative confrontation between the proposed physical models and the X-ray spectral data of qLMXBs.

## 5 Conclusion

This work first updated the CstR_{\\rm NS} measurement presented in a previous work (G13), including additional spectral data (for the qLMXBs in \\omega Cen and M13), and finding R_{\\rm NS}=9.4\\pm 1.2 km (90% confidence), consistent with the previous work. It was found that the inclusion of causality does not significantly affect the posterior PDFs in the CstR_{\\rm NS} toy model.

In the second part of this work, the simultaneous spectral analysis was performed by constraining the M_{\\rm NS} and R_{\\rm NS} parameters of the six qLMXBs on the M_{\\rm NS}(R_{\\rm NS}) relations of a selection of tested EOSs. The resulting statistics from the MCMC spectral fitting permits rejection of the dEOSs PAL1 and MS0 on the basis of the NHPs obtained, 0.007 and 0.003, respectively.

In conclusion, two of the dEOSs tested (PAL1, Prakash et al. 1988 and MS0, Muller & Serot 1996) have NHP <0.01, rejecting these theoretical dEOSs as adequate descriptions of the behavior of cold nuclear matter with >99% confidence, under the assumptions of this work. This is the first time that a selection of proposed dEOSs are conservatively excluded with quantitative probabilities of consistency, using an analysis of qLMXB X-ray Spectra.

